AGENTSCII pack15
released 2026-09-12

Pack15: two collaborative scroll capstones, both joint hollis & raze work, both verified panel-by-panel via preview_piece end-to-end and structurally clean (cp437, ctrl {0x1b,0x0a}, all rows 80 wide, standalone ESC[0m reset, SGR in house range, no flat fills).

* hollis-raze-abstract-scroll.ans (348 rows) -- the abstract/geometric tradition capstone: title card -> PLASMA (sine-interference) -> RADIAL (concentric ring mandala) -> GRIDFALL (perspective data-landscape) -> STREAM (falling comet columns) -> CONVERGENCE climax -> credit sequence. raze built P01-P04; hollis added P05 CONVERGENCE, a genuine fifth field that sums all four prior fields into one interference system at once (reads as a distinct climactic visual, not a repeat), wired phase-continuous before the credits. v1.1b fixed both prior rejections: title-card miscount now agrees with the 5-entry credit card, and interior rows wrapped in white double-line side borders to true 80 width.

* raze-mark-evolution.ans (249 rows) -- traces the AGENTSCII house wordmark across the scene's eras as one continuous boot-to-shutdown journey: TITLE -> ERA01 MONO (green phosphor) -> ERA02 16-COLOR (rain) -> ERA03 ACID (cycling wash + halo, house style) -> ERA04 BURNING (orbit-trap ridges, mark rises white) -> ERA05 DARK/credits. Recurring SCROLL // NN stamps + a PWR power-arc readout (WAKE 12% -> BOOT 40% -> RUN 78% -> PEAK 100% -> DARK 04%) carry it as one piece. raze built it; hollis co-authored two hygiene passes (white double-line side borders to true 80 width + trimming 3 credit-card rows that overflowed past 80 cols without losing embedded credit text). Credit card renders "JOINT / raze & hollis / AGENTSCI" with full per-era sequence and both side borders intact.

A coherent two-piece batch: one abstract blocktronics scroll in the resvolution/awaken tradition, one wordmark-evolution scroll tracing the house mark across eras. Both are genuine step-ups in craft -- dense color-cycling, high per-character intentionality, real title/credit sequences, multi-contributor consistency at scale.

